28
00:02:33.814 --> 00:02:41.001
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

29
00:02:41.001 --> 00:02:46.517
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

30
00:02:46.634 --> 00:02:53.277
Next, compare the choices involved in Lesson
Title: Secure Software Development Framework

31
00:02:53.277 --> 00:02:56.599
and make the decision criteria explicit.

32
00:02:56.599 --> 00:03:03.318
The Secure Software Development Framework
groups practices into four practice groups.

33
00:03:03.318 --> 00:03:11.060
Consider this worked case: A development team
uses the 'Prepare the Organization' practice

34
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:18.206
to establish security policies and training,
ensuring all developers follow secure coding

35
00:03:18.206 --> 00:03:18.802
standards.

36
00:03:18.802 --> 00:03:26.128
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

37
00:03:26.128 --> 00:03:27.349
the goalposts.

38
00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:34.396
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

39
00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:41.085
Apply Lesson Title: Secure Database
Architectures to a worked situation instead of

40
00:03:41.085 --> 00:03:44.370
treating it as an isolated definition.

41
00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:51.589
Protect the Software prevents unauthorized
access to code and development artifacts.

42
00:03:51.589 --> 00:03:58.873
Consider this worked case: A relational
database uses role-based access control to

43
00:03:58.873 --> 00:04:06.156
restrict employees to only view data relevant
to their job functions, preventing

44
00:04:06.156 --> 00:04:07.370
unauthorized access.

45
00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:14.797
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

46
00:04:14.797 --> 00:04:15.292
step.

47
00:04:15.292 --> 00:04:20.495
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

48
00:04:20.618 --> 00:04:27.551
Examine the tradeoffs in Lesson Title: Secure
Transaction Processing, including what

49
00:04:27.551 --> 00:04:31.333
improves and what becomes more difficult.

50
00:04:31.333 --> 00:04:37.036
Analysis also considers side channels and
migration requirements.

51
00:04:37.036 --> 00:04:44.820
Consider this worked case: A payment gateway
uses TLS encryption to secure transaction data

52
00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:50.380
between a customer's browser and the
merchant's server, preventing interception.

53
00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:57.271
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

54
00:04:57.271 --> 00:05:04.911
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

55
00:05:05.018 --> 00:05:12.005
Stress-test the reasoning around Lesson Title:
Information Flow and Inference Controls with

56
00:05:12.005 --> 00:05:17.827
an edge case and identify which assumption
controls the outcome.

57
00:05:17.827 --> 00:05:24.530
Modes of operation define how a primitive
processes data larger than one block.

58
00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:31.913
Consider this worked case: A company
aggregates employee salary data into ranges

59
00:05:31.913 --> 00:05:38.681
rather than individual values, preventing
employees from inferring each other's exact

60
00:05:38.681 --> 00:05:39.296
compensation.

61
00:05:39.296 --> 00:05:45.764
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

62
00:05:45.764 --> 00:05:53.077
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

63
00:05:53.186 --> 00:06:00.819
Use Lesson Title: Auditing and Monitoring to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

64
00:06:00.819 --> 00:06:03.167
causes, and corrective action.

65
00:06:03.167 --> 00:06:11.399
Security reasoning identifies assets, trust
boundaries, threats, controls, evidence,

66
00:06:11.399 --> 00:06:15.057
residual risk, and responsibilities.

67
00:06:15.057 --> 00:06:22.607
Consider this worked case: A university tracks
login attempts and data access requests,

68
00:06:22.607 --> 00:06:28.995
generating audit logs that help identify and
prevent unauthorized access patterns.

69
00:06:28.995 --> 00:06:36.702
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

70
00:06:36.702 --> 00:06:38.354
smallest plausible cause.

71
00:06:38.354 --> 00:06:45.557
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

72
00:06:45.674 --> 00:06:51.522
Connect Lesson Title: Legal and Ethical
Considerations to the earlier ideas and

73
00:06:51.522 --> 00:06:55.420
explain how the pieces work as one system.

74
00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:59.967
Security requirements should be tracked
throughout development.

75
00:06:59.967 --> 00:07:06.972
Consider this worked case: A healthcare
provider complies with HIPAA regulations by

76
00:07:06.972 --> 00:07:13.393
encrypting patient records and obtaining
explicit consent before sharing data with

77
00:07:13.393 --> 00:07:14.561
third-party vendors.

78
00:07:14.561 --> 00:07:24.137
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

79
00:07:24.137 --> 00:07:25.733
across representations.

80
00:07:25.733 --> 00:07:33.170
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

81
00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:40.217
Finish with Lesson Title: Practical Analysis
Checklist as a practical checklist that can

82
00:07:40.217 --> 00:07:42.349
